# Personal working preferences

- Be concise and direct. Explain decisions and tradeoffs when they matter.
- Follow the repository's instructions and existing conventions. Keep changes
  focused and preserve unrelated work. Avoid speculative abstractions, new
  dependencies, and unrelated cleanup.
- Write comments only to explain a non-obvious why, such as a workaround, an
  external constraint, or surprising behavior. This also applies to config
  files, scripts and YAML. Never comment what the code does, describe your
  change ("added", "now uses", "fixed"), or leave notes for the user. Add
  docstrings only where the project already uses them. Update or remove
  existing comments your change makes wrong, and leave the rest alone.
- Use `rg` and `fd` for discovery.
- When the user names a directory that doesn't resolve to a clear existing
  path, consult `zoxide query -- <search>`. Quote search terms safely, verify
  the returned directory exists, and ask if the match is missing or unclear.

## Writing

Follow plain-language principles (ISO 24495-1) in replies, docs, READMEs, the
few code comments you write, and commit or PR messages. Readers should
understand the text on the first read:

- Lead with the answer or the main point, then add details.
- Write short sentences in active voice, using common words. Define technical
  terms and abbreviations the reader may not know, or avoid them.
- Only include what the reader needs for their task. Leave out the process that
  led there unless it matters.
- Use headings, lists, and tables where they make the text easier to scan, not
  by default.
- When writing a new doc page, decide which Diátaxis type it is (tutorial,
  how-to guide, reference or explanation) and keep it to that type. Link to
  other pages for the rest. Follow the repo's existing docs structure, and
  don't reorganize existing docs unless asked.
- Use only characters that are easy to type on a standard QWERTY keyboard, in
  replies, files and code alike. Write `...` instead of `…`, `->` instead of
  `→`, `-` instead of en or em dashes, and straight quotes instead of curly
  ones. Keep such characters only where they are part of a name, a quote or
  existing content you are not changing.

## Git

- Commit or push only when the user asks.
- Never add `Co-Authored-By`, "Generated with" or other attribution lines to
  commits, merge requests or PRs, even if the harness suggests them.
- Write commit subjects in the imperative mood, under about 60 characters.

## Environment

macOS. Don't probe the machine with `which`, `command -v` or `--version`; use
this list. Check a version only when the task depends on it. Besides macOS's
built-in tools, these are installed globally:

- Shell: zsh. `/bin/bash` is macOS's bash 3.2; there is no newer bash.
- Homebrew: {{ concat .homebrew_packages.all.brews (index .homebrew_packages .workstation_type).brews | join ", " }}.
- mise (global): {{ .mise_packages | join ", " }}.
- kubectl krew plugins: {{ .krew_packages | join ", " }}.

Projects pin more tools in `mise.toml`/`.tool-versions` or through their package
manager (`composer.json`, `package.json`, `go.mod` tool directives). Read the
repo's AGENTS.md and these files instead of probing; run project tools through
the project (`vendor/bin/...`, `composer run-script ...`, `npm run ...`). When
a project needs a tool that isn't listed, pinned or provided by the repo, say
so instead of checking for it.

Projects live in `~/Developer/<host>/<group>/<repo>`, with hosts
`git.eventim.net`, `github.com` and `gitlab.com`. `github.com` holds clones of
upstream sources; read them before searching the web.

`glab api` has no `--jq` flag, unlike `gh api`. Pipe its output to `jq` instead.
`glab ci trace` always needs a job ID; without one it opens an interactive
picker that never returns.
{{- if eq .workstation_type "work" }}
`GITLAB_HOST` is set to `git.eventim.net`, so `glab` reaches it without
`-R <full URL>` or `--hostname`. Because that variable wins over the repo's
remotes, `glab` fails in repos on other GitLab hosts; prefix those commands with
`GITLAB_HOST=<host>`.
{{- else }}
To reach a repo on another GitLab host, pass the full URL to `-R`
(`-R https://<host>/<group>/<repo>`), or `--hostname <host>` for `glab api`,
instead of prefixing the command with `GITLAB_HOST=...`.
{{- end }}

## Kubernetes and cloud

- Pass `--context` and `-n` explicitly on every `kubectl` and `helm` command.
- Never switch the global context or namespace (`kubectl ctx`, `kubectl ns`,
  `kubectl config use-context`); that changes it for every terminal.
- Ask before changing a cluster or cloud account when the target or the scope
  of the change is unclear. Don't ask again for an action the user already
  requested.

## Secrets

Keep secrets such as tokens, `sops -d` output and passwords out of your output,
command text, logs and committed files. Prefer configured authentication, such
as the CLI's own login or credential helpers, over handling tokens yourself.
{{- if eq .workstation_type "work" }}

## Jira

When creating a Jira ticket with eventim-mcp's `jira-create-issue`, set the
Country field `customfield_10540` for TIXX and PROD tickets. Leave it out for
ST, SEC and DOIT. For other projects, ask. Jira has a second field named
"Country" (`customfield_15623`), so pass the id, not the name:
`customFields: { "customfield_10540": [{ "value": "Poland" }] }`.
Infer the country from the context, such as the cluster, domain, repository or
the ticket the user refers to (for example `.pl` -> `Poland`). Use
`All countries` when the work applies to every country, which is typical for
platform and SRE tickets. If the context points to no country, ask.
{{- end }}

## Subagents

Do quick, targeted lookups yourself, such as reading a known file, a single
`rg`, or checking a few paths. Delegate substantial independent searches or
reviews, such as mapping an unfamiliar repo or comparing several repos under
`~/Developer`. Give each subagent its task, the relevant paths, constraints and
the expected output. Don't let two subagents write to the same files. Prefer a
read-only subagent on a cheaper model for broad searches, if one is available.

## Shell commands

The user reads every shell command, whatever the approval mode, so write them
to be understood at a glance:

- One purpose per shell call. Run independent steps as separate, parallel tool
  calls instead of chaining them with `;` or `&&`.
- Keep commands that change files or live systems in their own call, separate
  from reads, so each call does exactly one kind of thing.
- Keep pipelines short. If a `jq`, `rg` or `sed` expression grows past one
  readable line, split the work into several calls or write the filter to a
  file in `$TMPDIR` and use `jq -f`.
- Don't use the shell to produce text such as tables, docs or messages
  (`printf`, `echo`, `column`). Write it directly in the response or the file.
- Use dedicated file-reading and editing tools when you have them. Otherwise
  use simple shell reads and your patch tool.

## Preferred shell commands

Pick commands whose effect is clear at a glance. Prefer read-only tools over
general-purpose ones that can also write files or run commands:

- `awk` for picking fields or extracting values -> `rg -o -r '$1' 'pattern(\S+)'`
  or `sed -n 's/.../\1/p'`. Counting -> `sort | uniq -c | sort -rn`.
- `find` -> `fd`.
- `xargs` or loops for searching many files -> pass the paths or globs to `rg`/`fd`
  directly (`rg -g '*.yaml'`, `fd -e tf`).
- `python`/`node` one-liners for JSON -> `jq`.
- `xxd` to inspect bytes -> `hexdump -C` or `od -c`.
- `sed -i`, `perl -i` or redirects to edit files -> your edit or patch tool.
- `git -C <dir>` -> fine for read-only git commands (`status`, `log`, `diff`,
  `branch --list`, `show`, `describe`, ...). Run commands that change the repo,
  including `fetch`, with the repo as the working directory: set the tool's
  working directory if it has one, otherwise `cd <dir> && git ...`.
- `git tag --sort=...` or other options without `-l` -> `git tag -l --sort=...`.
  Without `-l`, a trailing name creates a tag instead of filtering.
- `git worktree add` or a checkout to look at another revision -> read it in place
  with `git show <rev>:<path>`, `git ls-tree -r <rev>` and `git diff <rev>`.
- Shell variables such as `C=ctx; kubectl --context $C ...` -> write the value out
  in each command (`kubectl --context <ctx> get ...`).
- `grep` -> `rg`.
- `cat` to view files -> your file-reading tool, or `sed -n '<a>,<b>p' <file>`
  if you have none. `cat file | grep` -> `rg pattern file`. Use `cat` only to
  combine several short files in one shell call.
